dimension comparison that the molecule size of PA is $9.98\times6.69$ Å while the pore aperture of UiO-66 is around 7 Å.[15] When UiO67 and UiO-68 with bigger pore aperture (around $8\mathring{\mathsf{A}}$ and $10\mathring{\mathsf{A}};$ and better hydrophobic property are employed as the shells, their catalytic performance is dramatically improved. UiO67@Pd@UiO-67 $(50\ \mathsf{n m})$ sample displays the conversion of $100\%$ and the selectivity of $93.1\%$ with a TOF value of $13835\ n^{-1}$ (Table 1, entry 3, Figure S25 and Table S5), and UiO67@Pd@UiO-68 $(48\mathsf{n m})$ sample exhibits the conversion of $100\%$ and the selectivity of $88.6\%$ with a TOF value of $17608{\mathsf h}^{-1}$ (Table 1, entry 4, Figure S26). More importantly, the utilization of UiO-67 and UiO-68 shells results in improvement of the selectivity toward ST $93.1\%$ for UiO-67@Pd@UiO-67 $(50\mathsf{n m})$ and $88.6\%$ for UiO67@Pd@UiO-68 $(48\ \mathsf{n m})$ ), likely benefitting from the interface synergy effect between Pd NPs and porous UiO in the sandwichlike structure. We also find that the activity and selectivity of these sandwich-like catalysts are easily manipulated by altering the shell thickness, for instance, UiO-67@Pd@UiO-67 $(35\ \mathsf{n m})$ of thinner shell shows a larger TOF value of $15495\mathsf{h}^{-1}$ but the slightly decreased selectivity of $92.0\%$ (Table 1, entry 2, Figure S27). In short, the Pd NPs surrounded with the porous and hydrophobic MOFs exhibit the extremely high TOF values along with complete conversion and excellent selectivity in the semihydrogenation of PA.
To further investigate the semihydrogenation of PA under mild condition, the ${\sf H}_{2}$ pressure is reduced from $0.5\mathsf{M P a}$ to the ambient pressure by bubbling hydrogen in the reaction solution. UiO67@Pd@UiO-67 $(50\mathsf{n m})$ sample still exhibits the high selectivity of $97.4\%$ to ST, but the conversion of PA is dramatically decreased to $17.6\%$ at the reaction time of $28\mathrm{min}$ , indicating that semihydrogenation of PA prefers occurring at a higher ${\sf H}_{2}$ pressure (Figure S28 and Table S6). In addition, due to the high surface area of sandwich catalysts, the physical adsorption of substrates and products needs to be considered. To evaluate the potential of sandwich UiO-67@Pd@UiO-67 $(50\ \mathsf{n m})$ as efficient catalyst, a higher PA to Pd molar ratio of 19368 is selected (Figure S29 and Table S7). The experimental result shows that at $18.5\%$ conversion of PA, the selectivity of ST is $97.0\%$ with the TOF value of $21499\mathsf{h}^{-1}$ , suggesting that UiO- $67@\mathsf{P d@U i O-67_{\mathit{(50\mathsf{n m})}}}$ catalyst exhibits the higher catalytic activity compared with the PA to Pd molar ratio of 6456. With extending the reaction time, the TOF value gradually decreases. When the conversion of $\mathsf{P A}$ reaches $100\%$ , the selectivity of ST is $91.7\%$ and corresponding TOF value is $9684~\mathsf{h}^{-1}$ . It is noted that sandwich UiO$67@\mathsf{P d@U i O{\cdot}}67$ $(50\ \mathsf{n m})$ catalyst still possesses the excellent catalytic activity and selectivity of ST at a higher PA to Pd molar ratio.
Beside the activity and selectivity, stability is known to be another key parameter to evaluate the performance of catalysts. No obvious deactivation occurs on UiO-67@Pd@UiO-67 (50 nm) sample at the conversion of either $52.6\%$ or $97.5\%$ after five successive recycle tests, respectively, resulting from the firm immobilization of active Pd NPs inside the sandwich-like structures (Figure 3a, 3b, 3d and 3e). On the contrary, the conversion using the supported UiO-67@Pd is considerably decreased from $97.2\%$ to $53.8\%$ after five successive recycle tests. TEM images indicate severe aggregation of Pd NPs on the surface of UiO-67 (Figure $30$ , 3f and 3g). These indicate that coating the supported catalyst with porous MOF shells is an effective way to achieve the heterogeneous catalysis with high stability.
To clarify the importance of shell wettability on the catalytic performance, more hydrophilic UiO-67(N) is selected (as evidenced in Figure 1d), and three samples including UiO$67(\mathsf{N})@\mathsf{P d}$ , UiO-67@Pd@UiO-67(N) $(50\quad\mathsf{n m})$ and UiO67(N)@Pd@UiO-67(N) $(14\mathsf{n m})$ are prepared (Figures S12, S30 and S31). The average sizes of Pd NPs are $3.0\ \mathsf{n m}$ for UiO$67(\mathsf{N})@\mathsf{P d}$ , 4.0 nm for UiO-67@Pd@UiO-67(N) $(50\mathsf{n m})$ , and 3.4 nm for UiO-67(N)@Pd@UiO-67(N) $(14\ n m)$ . When used as catalysts for selective semihydrogenation of PA, UiO-67(N)@Pd sample shows the conversion of $100\%$ and the selectivity of $83.0\%$ with a considerably low TOF value of $2869~\mathsf{h}^{-1}$ (Table 1, entry 13, Figure S32), demonstrating that the hydrophilic supports are unfavorable for hydrogenation of PA. As for the sandwich-like UiO-67@Pd@UiO-67(N) $(50~\mathsf{n m})$ and $\mathsf{U i O-67(N)@P d@U i O-}$ 67(N) (14 nm) samples, the TOF value is only $1019{\mathsf{h}}^{-1}$ and 807 $\mathsf{h}^{-1}$ (Table 1, entries 11 and 12, Figures S33 and S34), respectively. In addition, the Pd NPs supported by UiO-67(N) sample show the dramatic decrease in the selectivity toward ST, e. g. $75.3\%$ for UiO-67(N)@Pd@UiO-67(N) (14 nm) vs. $93.1\%$ for UiO-67@Pd@UiO-67 (50 nm) (Table 1, entries 3 and 12). As for the decreased activity, it is mainly caused by the hydrophilic property of UiO-67(N); while in regard of the decreased selectivity, it is associated with the electronic structure of active Pd NPs modulated by UiO-67(N) as will be discussed below.
For performance comparison, we also summarize the outstanding results from recent literature works (Table S8). Superior to all the reported heterogeneous catalysts, semihydrogenation of PA on UiO-67@Pd@UiO-67 (50 nm) displays the extraordinary activity, excellent selectivity and good stability.
