X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS) was performed to inspect the interfacial interaction between Pd NPs and UiO supports. As for pure Pd NPs, characteristic Pd $3d_{5/2}$ binding energy at $335.66\:\mathsf{e V}$ is assigned to zero-valent Pd in the metallic state.[22] With respect to UiO-67@Pd sample, Pd $3d_{5/2}$ binding energy shifts toward a higher value of $335.93\ \mathrm{~eV}$ , and the corresponding binding energy of $Z r~3d_{5/2}$ shifts toward a lower value of 182.74 eV compared with that of pure UiO-67 sample (Figure 4a and 4b, and Figure S35a). Similarly, $\mathsf{P d}3d_{5/2}$ binding energy of UiO-66@Pd and UiO-68@Pd shifts toward higher value of 336.06 eV and $335.86~\mathsf{e V}$ , and the corresponding binding energy of $Z r~3d_{5/2}$ shifts toward lower value of $182.81~\mathsf{e V}$ and $182.96~\mathsf{e V}$ compared with pure UiO-66 and UiO-68, respectively (Figures S36 and S37). These suggest that there is electron transfer from Pd NPs to UiO-66, UiO-67 or UiO-68. Moreover, O 1s binding energy shifts toward a lower value for UiO-67@Pd with respect to that in pure UiO counterparts due to the strong interfacial interaction between Pd NPs and Zr nodes in UiO-67 (Figure 4c, and Figure S38a).[23]
In sharp contrast, $\mathsf{P d}\ 3d_{5/2}$ binding energy of UiO-67(N)@Pd sample shifts to a lower value of 334.60 eV compared with pure Pd NPs (Figure S35b), while the corresponding binding energy of
N 1s shifts to a higher value of $399.76~\mathsf{e V}$ with respect to pure UiO-67(N) (Figure 4d-4f). The interfacial electron transfer from UiO-67(N) to Pd NPs occurs in $\mathsf{U i O-}67(\mathsf{N})\ @\mathsf{P d}$ , because the lonepair electrons of N in UiO-67(N) frameworks prefer moving to lowlying bonding states (Figure S38b). Altogether, the electronic structure of Pd NPs could be effectively manipulated by grafting different functional organic linkers into UiO frameworks.
To elucidate the effect of electronic structure of Pd NPs on the selectivity of ST, DFT calculations were done on the neutral and charged Pd(111) surfaces, which present the most exposed facets of Pd NPs (Figure 2f), using a four-layered $(3\times3)$ supercell composed of $36{\mathsf{P d}}$ atoms (see the detailed calculation method in the Supporting Information). The most stable adsorption of ${\sf H}_{2}$ , PA, ST and EB on the neutral Pd(111) surface is compared. $H_{2}$ adsorbs on the neutral $\mathsf{P d}$ surface in a dissociation mode and H atom prefers attaching at the hollow site with an adsorption energy of $-2.63\mathrm{~\eV}$ (Figure S39a). The aromatic rings of adsorbates are strongly bonded to the Pd surface with a distance of $2.3\mathring{\mathsf{A}}$ , while the H atoms on benzene rings lift away from the surface, which makes C-H bonds a hybridization transition from $s p^{2}$ to $s p^{3}$ (Figure S39b). Interestingly enough, the alkynyl group of PA and the alkenyl group of ST exhibit different adsorption configuration on the Pd surface. The alkynyl group of PA is bonded tightly to the Pd surface with a distance of $2.1\mathring{\mathsf{A}}$ , which is even closer than that of the aromatic ring to the surface; meanwhile, the hydrogen atom of the alkynyl group is $50^{\circ}$ away from the surface, suggesting the C-H hybridization transition from $s p$ to $s\rho^{2}$ . As for semihydrogenation product ST (Figure S39c), the distance between the alkenyl group of the ST and Pd surface is $2.2\mathring{\mathsf{A}}$ , more away from the surface than the alkynyl group of PA, implying that weaker bonding of the alkenyl group than the alkynyl group on the Pd surface. When ST is hydrogenated to EB, the alkyl group has no interaction with the Pd surface, suggesting its facile desorption (Figure S39d).
All the above results disclose that the positive ${\mathsf{P d}}(111)$ surface in $\mathsf{U i O-}67@\mathsf{P d}$ samples, which are caused by interfacial electron transfer from Pd NPs and UiO supports, contributes to highly selective formation of ST rather than EB. To further validate this deduction, the interfacial electron transfer between ${\mathsf{P d}}(111)$ and PA/ST is firstly analyzed. Bader charge analysis discloses that the electron transfer is from neutral Pd (111) surfaces to PA and ST in the adsorption structures, and correspondingly, the calculated electron transfer value is $0.43\textsf{e}$ for $\mathsf{P d}$ to PA and $0.29\textsf{e}$ for $\mathsf{P d}$ to ST (Figure S41). Then, the charges on the Pd surface ranging from -0.6 to 0.6 are systematically calculated for the adsorption/desorption of PA and ST (Figure S42). With increase in the negative charge on ${\mathsf{P d}}(111)$ surface, the adsorption of PA and ST becomes stronger; whereas it turns weaker with increase in the positive charge, meaning that the higher selectivity of ST is obtained. This analysis is consistent with recent observation about the adsorption of PA and ST on the surfaces of Ni-O(111) and Ni(111).[24] Note that that the order for selectivity of ST on different $\mathsf{U i O@P d}$ samples follows UiO-66@Pd (Sel.: $89.1\%$ ) $>$ UiO-67@Pd (Sel.: $88.1\%$ ) $>$ UiO-68@Pd (Sel.: $87.4\%$ ) (Table 1, entries 5-7), which is in good agreement with the binding energy shift order from Pd to UiO with respect to pure Pd NPs, i.e. $0.4\mathsf{e V}$ for $\mathsf{U i O-66@P d~>~}0.27\ \mathsf{e V}$ for $\mathsf{J i O-}67@\mathsf{P d}>0.2\ \epsilon$ V for UiO$68@\mathsf{P d}$ (Figures 4, S36 and S37).
To further distinguish the key factors influencing catalytic activity and selectivity, an electron-withdrawing but hydrophilic MOF-74 (contact angle of 24o),[2e] which is formed via coordination of $\mathsf{N i}^{2+}$ ion with 2,5-dihydroxyterephthalic acid and characteristic with pore size of 1.47 nm, is selected as the support (Figures S43- S45). The average size of Pd NPs in as-synthesized MOF$74@\mathsf{P d}$ is $4.4\:\mathsf{n m}$ . When MOF-74@Pd is used as hydrogenation catalyst, the conversion of PA and selectivity of ST are $100\%$ and $88.2\%$ with a TOF value of $3099~{\mathsf{h}}^{-1}$ (Table 1, entry 14, Figure S46). With respect to UiO-67@Pd, MOF-74@Pd displays much lower TOF value $3099{\mathsf{h}}^{-1}$ vs. $19368\mathsf{h}^{-1}$ ) but identical selectivity $(88.2\%$ vs. $88.1\%$ , ascribing to wettability mismatch between hydrophilic MOF-74 pores and hydrophobic substrates. Moreover, compared with UiO-67(N) $@{\mathsf{P d}}$ , MOF-74@Pd shows similar TOF value $(3099\mathsf{h}^{-1}$ vs. $2869\mathsf{h}^{-1}$ ) but considerably enhanced selectivity $88.2\%$ vs. $83.0\%$ ), attributing to interfacial electron transfer from Pd NPs and MOF-74 supports (Figure S45). These results validate the experimental observations on catalytic performance of UiO-67@Pd@UiO-X toward selective semihydrogenation of PA (Table 1).
Other common terminal alkynes including 4-ethynyltoluene ( $10.84\times6.69\mathring{\mathsf{A}}_{\prime}^{\prime}$ , cyclohexylethyne $(9.98\times6.72\:\mathring{\mathsf{A}})$ and 1-octyne 1 $11.63\ \times\ 5.03\ \mathring{\mathsf{A}})$ are employed as substrates for selective semihydrogenation reactions on UiO-67@Pd@UiO-67 $(50\ \mathsf{n m})$ (Figure S47). Remarkably, when the conversion is $100\%$ for 4- ethynyltoluene, cyclohexylethyne and 1-octyne, respectively, the corresponding selectivity is $89.2\%$ for 4-methylstyrene with a TOF value of $10194\ensuremath{\mathsf{h}}^{-1}$ , $89.6\%$ for cyclohexylethene with a TOF value of $12105\ensuremath{\mathsf{h}}^{-1}$ , and $93.2\%$ for 1-octene with a TOF value of 11068 $\mathsf{h}^{-1}$ (Table 1, entries 15-17, Figures S48-S50). When two typical alkynols including 4-pentyn-1-ol $(8.89\times4.80\mathrm{~\AA})$ and 2-methyl-3- butyn-2-ol $(7.74\times6.71\mathrm{~\AA~}$ are used as substrates and completely converted, the corresponding selectivity is $90.6\%$ for 4-penten-1- ol with a TOF value of $12912\mathsf{h}^{-1}$ , and $88.8\%$ for 2-methyl-3-buten2-ol with a TOF value of $27669~\mathsf{h}^{-1}$ (Table 1, entries 18 and 19, Figures S51 and S52). Last but not least, when two internal alkynes including 2-butyne $(7.31\times4.17\mathring{\mathsf{A}})$ and 3-hexyne (10.40 $\phantom{-}\times5.03\mathring{\mathsf{A}},$ ) are utilized as substrates and completely converted, the corresponding selectivity is $96.7\%$ for 2-butylene with a TOF value of $25825\mathsf{h}^{-1}$ , and $95.3\%$ for 3-hexene with a TOF value of 19368 $\mathsf{h}^{-1}$ (Table 1, entries 20 and 21, Figures S53 and S54). Again, Pd NPs encapsulated by hydrophobic UiO shells show the unprecedented catalytic performances in semihydrogenation of different alkynes compared with the reported heterogeneous catalysts (Table S8).
## Conclusion
In summary, semihydrogenation of different alkynes to alkenes is greatly accelerated via decoupling the functions of porous MOF supports at the molecular level. The hydrophobic UiO supports, via the incorporation of more benzene rings into the organic linkers, facilitate the enrichment of the organic alkynes around the active Pd NPs so as to promote the activity, while the electron transfer from Pd NPs to the electron-withdrawing UiO nodes leads to the weak adsorption of target alkene products over Pd NPs and consequently the enhanced selectivity. Thus, the unprecedented catalytic performances on selective semihydrogenation of multiple alkynes are acquired with UiO-67@Pd@UiO-67 (50 nm) sample, superior to all the reported heterogeneous catalysts (Table S8). The concept of on-demand molecular architecture of the supports will offer new venues for designing high-performance heterogeneous catalysts toward high value-added but challenging reactions.
